nwm - a small X11 window manager

The Cinnamon tweaks from linux-essentials (windows-snap, alttab-instant,
alttab-close, smart-close, drag-to-top maximize), without Cinnamon. Plain
Xlib + Xinerama + Xft, so it runs on any distro with Xorg.

Keys
	Super+arrows		snap ladder: halves, quarters, max, min
	Super+Shift+Left/Right	move window to the next monitor
	Alt+Tab, Alt+Shift+Tab	switcher with thumbnails; Left/Right move,
				Q closes the highlighted one
	Ctrl+Shift+W		close (terminals keep it for their tabs)
	Alt+F4, Super+Q		close
	Super+drag		move; drop on top = max, side = half, corner = quarter
	snap assist		snap to an empty half and the other windows show in the
				other half; click one, or arrows and Enter, to fill it
	Super+right drag	resize from the nearest edge/corner
	Super (tap)		launcher, tap again to close; Enter runs the typed text if
				nothing matches.
				Also lock, log out, suspend, restart, shut down.
	window edges		drag just outside the focused window to resize
	Ctrl+Alt+T		gnome-terminal
	Super+E			nemo
	Super+Shift+T/A/V/S	ocrclip, askclaude, voicetoggle, screenshot
	Super+L, Ctrl+Alt+L	lock (cinnamon-screensaver)
	Fn keys			volume, mute, mic mute, brightness; a box shows the level
	Ctrl+Alt+Delete		quit

Bar
	Bottom of each monitor, hidden until the pointer touches the bottom
	edge (AUTOHIDE in nwm.c). A button per window on that monitor (click
	focuses, click the focused one minimizes), memory and swap in use (swap
	only when there is some configured), battery level
	(⚡ on AC), a
	clock and a power button
	(lock, log out, suspend, restart, shut down). The first one
	also has status text, tray icons and a sound button (click for a
	volume slider, click its speaker to mute, right click for Cinnamon's
	sound settings). The status text is the root
	window name, set with xsetroot -name.

No title bars; apps with their own (Chrome, GTK) drag as usual.
Everything is configured at the top of nwm.c.

Build and try
	make
	make run		nested in Xephyr on :3, the real session is untouched
	sudo make install	adds "nwm" to the login screen, Cinnamon stays

Startup
	XDG autostart entries for "nwm:X-Cinnamon", so the same daemons and
	tray apps as under Cinnamon. Then ~/.config/nwm/autostart if it is
	executable; autostart.example adds the locker and the status text.
	nwm -n (make run) starts nothing.

Tray icons
	Symbolic icons are drawn in the bar's text colour. blueman asks for
	blueman-tray-symbolic, which Mint-Y lacks; link Adwaita's bluetooth
	ones under ~/.local/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/status/ as
	blueman-{tray,active,disabled}-symbolic.svg.

Updating
	kill -USR1 $(pidof nwm) restarts it in place, into whatever nwm is
	first on PATH now; windows and tray icons stay.

Touchpad swipes
	Optional. Not linked into nwm, and `make` does not build it. X does
	not deliver three-finger swipes, so gestures/gesture_detector.c opens
	the touchpad with libinput and sends the keys above. It talks to any
	touchpad and never opens a keyboard. It releases Alt when it exits.

	Three fingers sideways	holds the Alt+Tab switcher open; the selection
				follows the fingers, lifting picks, a cancelled
				swipe escapes. Left/Right stop at the ends.
	Three fingers up/down	Super+Up/Down, the snap ladder. Only if the
				sideways switcher did not open.
	Two fingers right	Alt+Left (Back), once, at the end of a
				deliberate horizontal scroll. Left does not
				trigger Forward. The app still receives the scroll.

	Short moves, diagonals and reversals are ignored. The numbers are
	in the source: 60 units to fire, the switcher shows after 8 and
	steps every 40.

	Build needs libinput and libudev (pkg-config). Sending keys needs
	xdotool.

	make gesture_detector
	make check-gestures	the self-test, no touchpad involved
	sudo make install-gestures

	That installs nwm-gestures on PATH, a udev rule that lets whoever is
	at the screen read touchpads, and an autostart entry for the nwm
	session only. The install reloads udev. Log in again so logind grants
	the ACL and autostart starts the daemon. Logging in alone does not
	apply a newly installed rule. Another gesture daemon, such as
	Touchégg, has to be stopped or it takes the swipes.

Not yet
	display menu, workspaces.

Could have
	Night light in nwm, through XRandR gamma like xsct: no xsct or
	shell loop, a fade, back on after a screen is plugged or rotated,
	a key to toggle it.

Around nwm (set up on tab, not part of the build)
	Turned off for nwm only: ~/.config/autostart copies of mintreport,
	mintupdate, Evolution-alarm-notify, remmina-applet and
	xfce4-power-manager with "nwm;" added to NotShowIn. XFCE and
	Cinnamon still start them. xfce4-power-manager would fight
	csd-power over the lid.

	Rotation: xrandr --output DSI-1 --rotate right in the autostart.
	Cinnamon's Display settings stay empty under nwm, they talk to
	muffin.

	Touchpad: the autostart copies Cinnamon's natural-scroll and
	tap-to-click onto every touchpad with xinput, and again within 3 s
	whenever the keyboard dock reconnects with the defaults.

	Night light: Cinnamon's does nothing under nwm, so the autostart
	runs xsct 1000 in the hours set for it there (now 20-11).

	blueman: org.blueman.general symbolic-status-icons true, plus the
	Adwaita links under Tray icons above, for a white icon.

	Notifications: xfce4-notifyd starts on D-Bus demand, nothing to do.

	Firefox (profile 77jrvo59.default-release):
	user.js	unloadOnLowMemory, unload after 5 min unseen, 64 MB
		memory cache, no AV1 (the N4100 decodes VP9/H.264 in
		hardware), userContent.css on. processCount and
		max_total_viewers are back at defaults; trimming them
		saved nothing.
	chrome/userContent.css
		YouTube: no comments, chat, end screen, Shorts, ambient
		glow, hover previews or animations; recommendations stay.
		X: no Premium box, Who to follow, Grok, communities, jobs
		or animations; news and trends stay.
	Neither saved much RAM; they cut CPU and clutter. Delete the two
	files to undo.
